# ThreadSanitizer suppression list for LogSquirl (#347, #482).
#
# Applied to every ctest run (cmake/CatchTestDiscoveryRunTest.cmake) and
# documented for standalone binary runs in BUILD.md. See
# docs/adr/0007-tsan-suppresses-onetbb-and-uninstrumented-qt-internals.md for
# how every finding of the CI job was sorted and why each rule below is safe.
#
# Syntax: https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/ThreadSanitizerSuppressions
#
# It holds no `race:` entry. TSan matches such an entry against every frame of
# a stack, callers included, and the frames of LogSquirl's own code run on top
# of Qt's event loop, QThreadPool and oneTBB's flow graph. An entry naming one
# of those hides a race in LogSquirl's code as well, so none is allowed. The
# oneTBB entries this file held (`tbb::detail::`, `tbb::flow::`) did exactly
# that: without them, ~2200 reports came out of the Search and indexing code
# that runs in TBB's flow graph nodes (#482). oneTBB is now built with TSan
# instead (3rdparty/CMakeLists.txt), which leaves none of them. The Qt entries
# (`QThreadPoolThread::run`, `QThreadPool::start`) could not match: the Qt of
# the CI container has no symbols for them.
#
# A `race_top:` entry matches only the frame that made a racing access, not its
# callers. One is allowed for a function that calls no code of LogSquirl's,
# with its reason next to it.
#
# oneTBB: threading_control::set_active_num_workers() calls
# get_threading_control() without the lock every other caller holds, so it
# reads the global threading_control while a TBB thread that ends drops the
# last reference and clears it. A bug of the oneTBB fork LogSquirl builds
# (variar/oneTBB c9be1ac); upstream oneTBB takes the lock there. It is reached
# when a tbb::global_control is destroyed (the TBB-worker test, main() at exit).
race_top:tbb::detail::r1::threading_control::get_threading_control
#
# --- Libraries that are not built with ThreadSanitizer (#482) ---------------
#
# Qt, GLib and glibc come prebuilt, without -fsanitize=thread. TSan does not see
# their code, only the calls it makes into TSan's interceptors: operator
# new/delete, malloc/free, memcpy/memmove, strdup, the pthread objects, file
# descriptors. What orders those calls (QMutex, QAtomic reference counts, GMutex,
# glibc's own locks) is invisible to it, so it reports them as data races
# between the library and itself.
#
# TSan offers no rule for "the racing access was made inside this library":
# `race:` matches callers too (see above), `called_from_lib:` also switches off
# the pthread synchronization the library does, which turned 1059 reports into
# 4079 in CI, and `ignore_noninstrumented_modules` ignores every interceptor
# call on Linux, LogSquirl's own included. So the ctest runner applies that
# rule itself (cmake/TsanReportFilter.cmake): a data race report is left out
# only when BOTH racing accesses were made by one of the libraries listed below.
# An access counts as the library's when, after TSan's interceptor frame and the
# frames of inlined Qt and C++ standard library headers, the next frame is in
# that library. A race with one access in LogSquirl's code still fails the case.
#
# One kind of access inlined into LogSquirl's binaries counts as Qt's as well:
# an atomic operation on the reference count of a QString, QByteArray or QList
# payload (QArrayData::ref/deref, Qt's implicit sharing). The thread that drops
# the last reference frees the payload inside QtCore, after an atomic decrement
# TSan does not see there.
#
# And one pair with an access in LogSquirl's code is left out: a slot Qt calls
# for a queued signal (QObject::event in QtCore below it) reading an argument
# QtCore copied for that call on the emitting thread (QMetaType::create in
# QtCore allocating it). Qt hands the copy over through its event queue, and
# nothing else holds it. The same read against any other block still fails.
# Its twin for a functor queued with QMetaObject::invokeMethod is left out too
# (#517): the calling thread writing the QCallableObject Qt's inlined header
# code builds for it (QMetaObject::invokeMethodCallableHelper<F>), against
# QtCore calling or destroying that QCallableObject<F> on the receiving thread
# (QObject::event or ~QQueuedMetaCallEvent in QtCore right below its impl).
# Where the receiving side names its functor type, it must be F.
#
# The lines starting with "#@uninstrumented" are read by that filter, not by
# TSan, for which they are comments. Each needs its reason next to it.
#
# QtCore: Qt allocates an event, a slot's argument array, a result or a shared
# string payload on one thread and frees or copies it on another, after
# handing it over through its event queue, QThreadPool or an atomic reference
# count, all inside QtCore.
#@uninstrumented libQt6Core.so.6
# QtGui: its font, image and platform caches are built on one thread and
# released on another, under QtCore's locks.
#@uninstrumented libQt6Gui.so.6
# QtNetwork: the host lookup behind the update check hands its result from its
# lookup thread to the main thread the same way.
#@uninstrumented libQt6Network.so.6
# GLib, Qt's event dispatcher on Linux: the eventfd a GMainContext wakes up
# through is made on one thread and written to from another, under a GMutex.
#@uninstrumented libglib-2.0.so.0
# glibc: mktime() and localtime() keep the parsed TZ under glibc's own lock and
# replace it (strdup, free) from whichever thread converts a time first.
#@uninstrumented libc.so.6
